2026-09-27T01:51:58Z authored HFE C282Y/C282Y conclusion='You have two copies of the HFE change known as C282Y, one from each parent. The HFE gene helps control how much iron the body absorbs from food. C282Y is the name lab reports use for a change of one DNA letter in the HFE gene that stops the protein it makes from working properly. This is the pattern found in most people diagnosed with hereditary haemochromatosis, where the body slowly stores too much iron, and about 1 in 200 people of northern European ancestry have it. It is well established that by middle age most people with it have higher iron levels in the blood than people with two normal copies. Far fewer become ill: in a large study following people from middle age for about 12 years, about 28 in 100 men and 1 in 100 women with this result developed iron-related disease, such as liver damage or joint damage in the knuckles. Women are thought to be protected partly by monthly blood loss. A blood test of ferritin and transferrin saturation shows whether iron is building up, and a doctor decides what, if anything, to do.' (judged; no value from PMIDs 18199861, 15858186, 19554541, 9138148, 21452290, 26153218 to disagree with) by=Anton Kulaga (with Claude) human_reviewed=false
2026-09-27T01:51:58Z authored HFE C282Y/H63D conclusion='You have one C282Y change and one H63D change in the HFE gene, one on each copy of the chromosome (in trans), so neither copy makes the usual HFE protein. The HFE gene helps control how much iron the body absorbs from food. C282Y is the name lab reports use for a change of one DNA letter in the HFE gene that stops the protein it makes from working properly. H63D is the name lab reports use for a common one-letter change in the HFE gene whose effect on iron is small. Your iron levels are likely to run somewhat higher than those of people with two normal copies, but illness from iron is rare: in a large study following people from middle age for about 12 years, 1 of 82 men and none of 95 women with this combination developed iron-related disease. When people with it do build up too much iron, it is usually mild to moderate and usually comes with something else, such as heavy drinking or a fatty liver. About 2 in 100 people of northern European ancestry have this combination. A blood test of ferritin and transferrin saturation shows how your body is handling iron, and a doctor decides what, if anything, to do. This is a different finding from carrying both changes on the same copy, which leaves one normal copy.' (judged; no value from PMIDs 19554541, 26153218, 11325323, 20008199 to disagree with) by=Anton Kulaga (with Claude) human_reviewed=false
2026-09-27T01:51:58Z authored HFE C282Y-H63D/wt conclusion='You carry the C282Y and H63D changes on the same copy of the HFE gene, and a normal version on the other copy. The HFE gene helps control how much iron the body absorbs from food. C282Y is the name lab reports use for a change of one DNA letter in the HFE gene that stops the protein it makes from working properly. H63D is the name lab reports use for a common one-letter change in the HFE gene whose effect on iron is small. This arrangement is very rare: expert guidelines state that the two changes arose separately in human history and are found on different copies of the gene. With one normal copy, this is expected to behave like carrying a single C282Y change, which does not raise the risk of hereditary haemochromatosis, although this arrangement has not been studied directly. If your DNA file cannot tell which arrangement you have, one change on each copy is by far the more likely one.' (judged; no value from PMIDs 26153218, 21452290 to disagree with) by=Anton Kulaga (with Claude) human_reviewed=false
2026-09-27T01:51:58Z authored HFE C282Y/wt conclusion='You carry one copy of the HFE change known as C282Y and one normal copy. The HFE gene helps control how much iron the body absorbs from food. C282Y is the name lab reports use for a change of one DNA letter in the HFE gene that stops the protein it makes from working properly. This is common: about 1 in 10 people of European ancestry are carriers, as measured in large North American studies. It is well established that carriers are not at increased risk of hereditary haemochromatosis. Some carriers have slightly higher iron levels than people with two normal copies, but illness from iron overload is very rare and, when it happens, other genetic or lifestyle factors may be involved. The main way it matters is for children: a child who inherits your C282Y and another C282Y from their other parent would have two copies, the pattern behind most hereditary haemochromatosis.' (judged; no value from PMIDs 11325323, 20008199, 21452290, 26153218 to disagree with) by=Anton Kulaga (with Claude) human_reviewed=false
2026-09-27T01:51:58Z authored HFE H63D/H63D conclusion='You have two copies of the HFE change known as H63D, one from each parent. The HFE gene helps control how much iron the body absorbs from food. H63D is the name lab reports use for a common one-letter change in the HFE gene whose effect on iron is small. Doctors do not count this result as a cause of hereditary haemochromatosis, where the body stores too much iron. Your iron levels may be slightly higher than those of people with two normal copies, but most people with this result never build up harmful amounts of iron, and whether it adds any risk on its own is not settled. About 2 to 3 in 100 people of European ancestry have this result. If iron overload is ever found, a doctor will look for other causes, such as a fatty liver or heavy drinking.' (judged; no value from PMIDs 26153218, 11325323, 20008199, 21452290 to disagree with) by=Anton Kulaga (with Claude) human_reviewed=false
2026-09-27T01:51:58Z authored HFE H63D/wt conclusion='You carry one copy of the HFE change known as H63D and one normal copy. The HFE gene helps control how much iron the body absorbs from food. H63D is the name lab reports use for a common one-letter change in the HFE gene whose effect on iron is small. This is very common: about 1 in 4 people of European ancestry carry it, as measured in a large North American study, and it is the most widespread HFE change worldwide. Carriers are not at risk of iron overload from this change, and no difference you would notice is known. It matters for children only in combination with C282Y, a stronger HFE change, from their other parent, and even that combination rarely causes illness.' (judged; no value from PMIDs 20008199, 26153218, 21452290 to disagree with) by=Anton Kulaga (with Claude) human_reviewed=false
2026-09-27T01:51:58Z authored HFE C282Y-H63D/C282Y conclusion='You have the C282Y change on both copies of the HFE gene, with an extra H63D change on one of them. The HFE gene helps control how much iron the body absorbs from food. C282Y is the name lab reports use for a change of one DNA letter in the HFE gene that stops the protein it makes from working properly. H63D is the name lab reports use for a common one-letter change in the HFE gene whose effect on iron is small. This arrangement is very rare: expert guidelines state that the two changes arose separately in human history and are found on different copies of the gene. It is expected to work like having two C282Y copies, the pattern behind most hereditary haemochromatosis, because the extra change sits on a copy that is already affected; this has not been studied directly. By middle age most people with two C282Y copies have raised iron levels, and far fewer become ill, men much more often than women. A blood test of ferritin and transferrin saturation shows whether iron is building up, and a doctor decides what, if anything, to do.' (judged; no value from PMIDs 26153218, 18199861 to disagree with) by=Anton Kulaga (with Claude) human_reviewed=false
2026-09-27T01:51:58Z authored HFE wt/wt conclusion='You have neither of the two common HFE changes linked with hereditary haemochromatosis. The HFE gene helps control how much iron the body absorbs from food. The two common changes linked with iron overload are known on lab reports as C282Y and H63D. This is the most common result, found in about 6 in 10 people of European ancestry in a large North American study, and it means you are not at increased risk of the common inherited form, where the body stores too much iron. It does not rule out rarer changes in the gene or other causes of high iron, so a blood test is still the way to check iron levels if there is a reason to.' (judged; no value from PMIDs 20008199, 26153218 to disagree with) by=Anton Kulaga (with Claude) human_reviewed=false
