2026-09-27T08:02:06Z override HFE C282Y/C282Y conclusion='You have two copies of the HFE change known as C282Y, one from each parent. The HFE gene helps control how much iron the body absorbs from food. C282Y is the name lab reports use for a change of one DNA letter in the HFE gene that stops the protein it makes from working properly. This is the pattern found in most people diagnosed with hereditary haemochromatosis, where the body slowly stores too much iron, and about 1 in 200 people of northern European ancestry have it. It is well established that by middle age most people with it have higher iron levels in the blood than people with two normal copies. Far fewer become ill: in a large study following people from middle age for about 12 years, about 28 in 100 men and 1 in 100 women with this result developed iron-related disease, which in that study counted raised liver enzymes (a blood sign that the liver is under strain) as well as liver damage or joint damage in the knuckles. Serious damage, such as scarring of the liver (cirrhosis), is much rarer: expert guidelines put it at about 4 in 100 people with this result, and between 5 and 6 in 100 men. Women are thought to be protected partly by monthly blood loss. Each of your brothers and sisters has about a 1 in 4 chance of having the same result. A blood test of iron levels (ferritin and transferrin saturation) shows whether iron is building up. If it is high, the usual treatment, regularly taking off some blood, is simple and prevents damage, and a doctor decides whether it is needed.' outranks PMIDs 18199861, 26153218, 21452290 ('You have two copies of the HFE change known as C282Y, one from each parent. The HFE gene helps control how much iron the body absorbs from food. C282Y is the name lab reports use for a change of one DNA letter in the HFE gene that stops the protein it makes from working properly. This is the pattern found in most people diagnosed with hereditary haemochromatosis, where the body slowly stores too much iron, and about 1 in 200 people of northern European ancestry have it. It is well established that by middle age most people with it have higher iron levels in the blood than people with two normal copies. Far fewer become ill: in a large study following people from middle age for about 12 years, about 28 in 100 men and 1 in 100 women with this result developed iron-related disease, such as liver damage or joint damage in the knuckles. Women are thought to be protected partly by monthly blood loss. A blood test of ferritin and transferrin saturation shows whether iron is building up, and a doctor decides what, if anything, to do.') by=Anton Kulaga (with Claude) human_reviewed=false [replaced an earlier record]
